WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.670
<v Maya>Welcome back to part two of Cardio Regulations.

2
00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:16.750
<v Maya>Last time we learned that cardio builds endurance, that cardiorespiratory endurance is the engine and muscular endurance is the tyres, and that aerobic cross training builds comprehensive endurance.

3
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.050
<v Dev>And today?

4
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.480
<v Maya>Two big topics.

5
00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:26.950
<v Maya>First, how to classify aerobic activities by the stress they put on joints, so you can always give a client the right activity.

6
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:32.150
<v Maya>Second, a revision of blood circulation, because the next terms and formulas all depend on it.

7
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.380
<v Maya>The goal of this topic is right client, right activity.

8
00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:44.470
<v Maya>Whatever stress an activity involves, or whatever stress a client already has, there should never be a day when you can't assign a suitable aerobic activity.

9
00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.020
<v Maya>A client should not leave empty handed.

10
00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:51.160
<v Dev>And aerobic here still means more than three minutes non stop?

11
00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.050
<v Maya>Yes.

12
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.790
<v Maya>Only aerobic activities are considered.

13
00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.310
<v Maya>There are three categories, each with rules.

14
00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.010
<v Maya>Category one is weight bearing, impact based, short form WBIB.

15
00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:05.350
<v Maya>It has three rules, and the activity must meet all three.

16
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.400
<v Maya>Rule A, the body is upright.

17
00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:12.160
<v Maya>Rule B, body weight comes on the key load bearing joints, the hip, knee and ankle.

18
00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:14.290
<v Maya>Rule C, there is a foot strike.

19
00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.500
<v Maya>The foot touches the surface, leaves it, touches again.

20
00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:19.070
<v Maya>On, off, on, off.

21
00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.260
<v Dev>So A and B make it weight bearing, and C makes it impact based.

22
00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:25.880
<v Maya>Exactly, but you check all three together.

23
00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:27.010
<v Maya>Examples.

24
00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.840
<v Maya>Walking, jogging and running, the same family at different pace.

25
00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:31.840
<v Maya>Skipping.

26
00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:36.270
<v Maya>Stair climbing, or the stair master machine, because each step is a foot strike.

27
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.270
<v Maya>Zumba and step aerobics.

28
00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:39.640
<v Maya>Combat fitness.

29
00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:40.990
<v Maya>And the treadmill.

30
00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:42.130
<v Dev>Hold on.

31
00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.380
<v Dev>The treadmill has a moving belt.

32
00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:46.480
<v Dev>Is that really a foot strike?

33
00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.390
<v Maya>This confuses many people.

34
00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.110
<v Maya>The platform doesn't matter.

35
00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.560
<v Maya>Road, beach, park or treadmill, foot strike is foot strike.

36
00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:59.320
<v Maya>The foot leaves the surface and lands again, the body is upright, and the weight is on hip, knee and ankle.

37
00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:01.990
<v Maya>So the treadmill is WBIB.

38
00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:05.440
<v Dev>What about jumping jacks, burpees and high knees?

39
00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.750
<v Dev>They're upright with a foot strike.

40
00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.940
<v Maya>They came up in class, but they're not purely aerobic.

41
00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:16.510
<v Maya>They contain an anaerobic element, and jumping jacks are counted in repetitions, not time.

42
00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.450
<v Maya>They fail the three minute rule.

43
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.750
<v Maya>Skipping with a rope, though, is aerobic and qualifies.

44
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:25.690
<v Maya>HYROX is not here either, because of the sled push and squats.

45
00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.420
<v Dev>And combat fitness?

46
00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:35.960
<v Maya>It was written with a question mark in the lecture and not formally settled, but it's in the WBIB list on the board and the slides, so keep it there.

47
00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:39.080
<v Dev>Does this classification help beyond joints?

48
00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:40.870
<v Maya>Yes, with personality.

49
00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:45.190
<v Maya>An introvert who dislikes group training can be given solo activities.

50
00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:53.140
<v Maya>An extrovert gets bored alone and would enjoy group activities like zumba, dance aerobics, step aerobics or combat fitness.

51
00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:57.750
<v Maya>Category two is weight bearing, non impact based, WBNIB.

52
00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:02.830
<v Maya>Rules A and B are copied from category one, upright and weight on hip, knee and ankle.

53
00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.430
<v Maya>Rule C changes.

54
00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:06.070
<v Maya>There is no foot strike.

55
00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.900
<v Maya>The feet stay fixed on a pedal or platform, and the pedal moves.

56
00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:11.590
<v Dev>What are the examples?

57
00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:12.790
<v Maya>All machines.

58
00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.650
<v Maya>The elliptical cross trainer.

59
00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.950
<v Maya>The arc trainer, whose pedals move in an arc, hence the name.

60
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:25.710
<v Maya>The adaptive motion trainer, which adapts to different motion styles, so it can feel like elliptical, jogging, mountain climbing or running.

61
00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.020
<v Maya>And the stepper machine, where two pedals move up and down.

62
00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.800
<v Dev>Isn't the stepper the same as step aerobics?

63
00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:34.010
<v Maya>No, and this is a classic trap.

64
00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:39.670
<v Maya>In step aerobics you hop on and off a step board, so there are foot strikes, and it's WBIB.

65
00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:45.190
<v Maya>On the stepper machine the foot stays on the pedal, so no foot strike, so WBNIB.

66
00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:50.240
<v Maya>The skier machine, which mimics skiing on snow, is also WBNIB.

67
00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:53.190
<v Dev>Why is the cross trainer called elliptical?

68
00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.300
<v Maya>A revision link to joint types.

69
00:03:55.350 --> 00:04:00.350
<v Maya>A condyloid joint, like the wrist, is also called ellipsoid, meaning oval shaped.

70
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.300
<v Maya>In a ball and socket joint the socket is round.

71
00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:05.590
<v Maya>In a condyloid joint it's oval.

72
00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.530
<v Maya>The pedals move in an oval path, so elliptical.

73
00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:13.680
<v Maya>Category three is non weight bearing, non impact based, NWBNIB.

74
00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:18.930
<v Maya>This is the low stress category, and all activities that didn't fit the first two end up here.

75
00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:23.280
<v Maya>The only rule is that the body is in water, or seated, or lying down.

76
00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:25.730
<v Dev>So swimming, rowing and cycling.

77
00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:26.620
<v Maya>Yes.

78
00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:28.450
<v Maya>Swimming, body in water.

79
00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.130
<v Maya>Rowing machine, seated.

80
00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.940
<v Maya>Cycling, which falls here by default because the body is seated.

81
00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:39.490
<v Maya>Ankle movement happens, but there's no foot strike and no body weight on the load bearing joints.

82
00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:41.460
<v Dev>There are different bikes, right?

83
00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:42.640
<v Maya>Five to know.

84
00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:46.450
<v Maya>The upright bike is the regular gym bike with a V shaped seat.

85
00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:53.160
<v Maya>The recumbent bike is lower, pedalling is towards the front, with a back rest, arm supports and a wide cushioned seat.

86
00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:56.110
<v Maya>The hand or arm bike is pedalled with the arms.

87
00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.730
<v Maya>The air bike has a big fan instead of a front wheel and works on wind resistance.

88
00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:04.180
<v Maya>And group indoor cycling, often called spinning classes.

89
00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:06.620
<v Dev>Who is the recumbent bike for?

90
00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:12.910
<v Maya>People with balance problems such as vertigo, and the slide adds epilepsy, feel more stable on the low seat.

91
00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:16.020
<v Maya>The back rest supports people with lower back stress.

92
00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:21.230
<v Maya>And the wide seat removes the groin discomfort some people feel on the V shaped seat.

93
00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:22.830
<v Dev>And the arm bike?

94
00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:28.630
<v Maya>Ideal for people with leg disabilities such as polio, or severe arthritis in the knees.

95
00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:36.440
<v Maya>The upper limbs contribute less to cardiorespiratory endurance than the legs, because legs have bigger muscles, bones and joints.

96
00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:38.630
<v Maya>But something is better than nothing.

97
00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:40.190
<v Dev>And the air bike?

98
00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:42.560
<v Maya>Resistance adjusts automatically.

99
00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:47.500
<v Maya>More effort, harder it feels, less effort, easier, because of the wind through the fan.

100
00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:50.790
<v Maya>And since the handles move too, it's cross training in a way.

101
00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:54.420
<v Maya>Rowing also uses upper and lower body, so it counts too.

102
00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:55.370
<v Maya>Recap.

103
00:05:55.420 --> 00:06:00.630
<v Maya>WBIB has three rules, upright, weight on hip, knee and ankle, and a foot strike.

104
00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:04.550
<v Maya>WBNIB keeps the first two and drops the foot strike.

105
00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:09.480
<v Maya>NWBNIB has one rule, in water, seated or lying down.

106
00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:11.180
<v Dev>A quick way to remember.

107
00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:13.420
<v Dev>Foot strike means category one.

108
00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:16.630
<v Dev>Upright but no foot strike means category two.

109
00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:19.890
<v Dev>Water, seated or lying means category three.

110
00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:20.900
<v Maya>Perfect.

111
00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:22.650
<v Maya>And the rules never change.

112
00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:28.300
<v Maya>If a brand new machine is launched ten years from now, you can still place it using the same three rules.

113
00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:29.500
<v Maya>Pause and think.

114
00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:32.500
<v Maya>Which category is the stepper machine, and why?

115
00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:34.530
<v Dev>WBNIB.

116
00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:40.020
<v Dev>Upright, weight on hip, knee and ankle, but the foot stays on the pedal, so no foot strike.

117
00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:41.010
<v Maya>Correct.

118
00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:42.780
<v Maya>And the recumbent bike?

119
00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:46.540
<v Dev>NWBNIB, because the body is seated.

120
00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:47.420
<v Maya>Exactly.

121
00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:50.580
<v Maya>Now the walk along revision of blood circulation.

122
00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:52.640
<v Maya>Draw three boxes in your mind.

123
00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:54.460
<v Maya>Lungs, with alveoli.

124
00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:58.920
<v Maya>The heart with four chambers, two upper atria and two lower ventricles.

125
00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:00.600
<v Maya>And the cells of the body.

126
00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:07.170
<v Maya>In the drawing, the heart is shown in the anatomical position, so the right chambers appear on the left of the page.

127
00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:09.130
<v Dev>Any rules to remember?

128
00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:10.020
<v Maya>Four.

129
00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:12.470
<v Maya>Atrium, left or right, is inflow.

130
00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:15.000
<v Maya>Ventricle, left or right, is outflow.

131
00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:17.020
<v Maya>Arteries go away from the heart.

132
00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:18.930
<v Maya>Veins come towards the heart.

133
00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:22.930
<v Maya>Red means oxygenated blood, blue means deoxygenated.

134
00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:24.440
<v Dev>Start walking.

135
00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:30.380
<v Maya>Oxygenated blood leaves the lungs through the pulmonary veins into the left atrium, which is inflow.

136
00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:34.600
<v Maya>It crosses the bicuspid valve, two flaps, into the left ventricle.

137
00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:44.600
<v Maya>From the left ventricle it goes out through the aortic valve into the aorta, the biggest artery, then to the arteries, medium sized, then the arterioles, the smallest.

138
00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:46.980
<v Maya>The sizes reduce along the way.

139
00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:48.210
<v Dev>And then?

140
00:07:48.260 --> 00:07:49.320
<v Maya>Capillaries.

141
00:07:49.370 --> 00:07:51.130
<v Maya>The end point of contact.

142
00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:54.590
<v Maya>They wrap around the cells, and this is where exchange happens.

143
00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:59.070
<v Maya>Capillaries deliver oxygen, and the cells give back carbon dioxide.

144
00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:01.340
<v Maya>The lecture called it the return gift.

145
00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:05.080
<v Maya>Then the same capillaries carry deoxygenated blood.

146
00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:06.540
<v Dev>And the way back?

147
00:08:06.590 --> 00:08:09.510
<v Maya>The sizes are the same, but the flow is reversed.

148
00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:11.300
<v Maya>Small to medium to big.

149
00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:16.790
<v Maya>Capillaries to venules, the smallest veins, then veins, then the vena cava, the biggest.

150
00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:25.050
<v Maya>The vena cava splits into the superior vena cava, carrying blood from above the heart, and the inferior vena cava, from below the heart.

151
00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:29.800
<v Maya>The inferior one is a little thicker because more of the body lies below the heart.

152
00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:32.100
<v Dev>Then into the right atrium.

153
00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:33.670
<v Maya>Yes, inflow again.

154
00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:37.960
<v Maya>Then across the tricuspid valve, three flaps, into the right ventricle.

155
00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:42.740
<v Maya>From there across the pulmonary valve, into the pulmonary arteries, to the lungs.

156
00:08:42.790 --> 00:08:46.980
<v Maya>In the lungs the alveoli, tiny air sacs, do gaseous exchange.

157
00:08:47.030 --> 00:08:51.530
<v Maya>Carbon dioxide leaves, oxygen is added, and the cycle continues.

158
00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:54.780
<v Dev>Tricuspid has three, bicuspid has two.

159
00:08:54.830 --> 00:08:55.670
<v Maya>Yes.

160
00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:58.780
<v Maya>The valves' job is to make sure there is no backflow.

161
00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:00.390
<v Maya>One way traffic.

162
00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:02.940
<v Dev>Is there a trick for the word pulmonary?

163
00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:07.090
<v Maya>Whenever the lungs are directly involved, the word pulmonary appears.

164
00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:10.740
<v Maya>Pulmonary valve, pulmonary arteries, pulmonary veins.

165
00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:12.920
<v Maya>And there are two exceptions to remember.

166
00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:18.220
<v Maya>The pulmonary arteries go away from the heart, so they are arteries, yet they carry blue blood.

167
00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:20.380
<v Maya>The only arteries with blue blood.

168
00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:24.220
<v Maya>The pulmonary veins come towards the heart, yet carry red blood.

169
00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:26.470
<v Maya>The only veins with red blood.

170
00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:31.010
<v Dev>So the rule about arteries and veins is about direction, not colour.

171
00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:32.190
<v Maya>Exactly.

172
00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:34.900
<v Maya>The slides split this into two circulations.

173
00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:39.780
<v Maya>Pulmonary circulation is between the heart and the lungs, for oxygenating the blood.

174
00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:42.620
<v Maya>P because the lungs are directly involved.

175
00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:46.880
<v Maya>Systemic circulation is between the heart and the cells, the bigger cycle.

176
00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:52.270
<v Maya>S from system, because cells make tissues, tissues make organs, organs make systems.

177
00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:55.340
<v Dev>Two names for the two loops of one big cycle.

178
00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:57.250
<v Maya>Quick recap of circulation.

179
00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:03.600
<v Maya>Atrium inflow, ventricle outflow, arteries away, veins towards, and two loops, pulmonary and systemic.

180
00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:05.670
<v Maya>Here are the five things to remember.

181
00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:10.520
<v Maya>One, WBIB needs upright, weight on hip, knee and ankle, and a foot strike.

182
00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:17.660
<v Maya>Two, WBNIB has no foot strike, like the elliptical, arc trainer, adaptive motion trainer and stepper.

183
00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:23.380
<v Maya>Three, NWBNIB is water, seated or lying, like swimming, rowing and cycling.

184
00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:28.580
<v Maya>Four, atrium is inflow, ventricle is outflow, arteries away, veins towards.

185
00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:33.560
<v Maya>Five, pulmonary arteries carry blue blood and pulmonary veins carry red.

186
00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:36.940
<v Dev>Next, the key terms and formulas, and workload.

187
00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:38.240
<v Maya>See you in part three.

